IMPORT prelude

-- Supreme Court of Judicature Act 1969 (Singapore) -- civil appeal routes: appeals from the State Courts (s 21 and the
-- Third Schedule), decisions of the General Division that cannot be appealed (s 29 and the Fourth Schedule), that need
-- permission (s 29A and the Fifth Schedule), and which appellate court hears them (s 29C and the Sixth Schedule); the
-- rehearing powers (s 22(7)); and stays (s 60C).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Cited by 38 of the 527 Singapore Acts deposited in this repository: s 80 (Rules of Court) by 25 and
-- s 29C (which court hears an appeal) by 11. SCOPED to the civil appeal routes, which is what a litigant needs and what
-- s 29C's citers rely on. Not encoded: the courts' constitution and officers, original jurisdiction, the Singapore
-- International Commercial Court, criminal appeals, post-appeal capital applications, civil restraint orders and
-- vexatious litigants, and s 80 itself (a rule-making power).
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Supreme Court of Judicature Act 1969 -- civil appeals`

§§ `21 -- from the District and Magistrates' Courts`

-- s 21(1): an appeal lies to the General Division from a District Court or Magistrate's Court "ONLY WITH THE PERMISSION
-- of that ... Court or the General Division" where "(a) the amount in dispute ... (excluding interest and costs) does not
-- exceed $60,000"; or "(b) any case specified in the Third Schedule". (2A): the General Division's order on permission
-- "is final".
-- Third Schedule: unconditional permission to defend; conditional permission to defend (unless the appellant is the
-- party made to pay); setting aside a default judgment unconditionally; setting one aside on condition (same exception).
DECLARE StateCourtOrder IS ONE OF
    `a final judgment`
    `unconditional permission to defend`
    `conditional permission to defend, appealed by the defendant`
    `conditional permission to defend, appealed by the claimant`
    `an unconditional setting aside of a default judgment`
    `a conditional setting aside, appealed by the party made to pay`
    `a conditional setting aside, appealed by the other party`

GIVEN `the order` IS A StateCourtOrder, `amount in dispute` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `permission is needed to appeal from the State Court` IF
        `amount in dispute` AT MOST 60000
    OR  CONSIDER `the order`
        WHEN `a final judgment` THEN FALSE
        WHEN `conditional permission to defend, appealed by the defendant` THEN FALSE
        WHEN `a conditional setting aside, appealed by the party made to pay` THEN FALSE
        OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `29, 29A and the Fourth and Fifth Schedules -- the General Division`

-- s 29 and Fourth Schedule para 1: NO appeal against, among others, (b) a judgment or order "made by consent"; (h) an
-- order giving or refusing further and better particulars; (i) an order GIVING permission to amend a pleading (save the
-- late, name-or-capacity or new-cause amendment); (j) an order REFUSING security for costs; (k) an order giving or
-- refusing interrogatories.
-- para 3: no appeal where "the parties have agreed in writing signed by or on behalf of each party that the decision is
-- final", unless the appellant proves fraud, illegality, or a fundamental breach of natural justice -- and then only with
-- permission (Fifth Schedule para 5). The agreement may be made BEFORE OR AFTER the decision.
-- s 29A(1)(b): PERMISSION is needed where the amount in dispute (excluding interest and costs) "does not exceed $250,000",
-- unless (Fifth Schedule para 2(2)) the General Division was exercising original jurisdiction a written law gives it
-- exclusively, or it is the Family Division at first instance.
-- Fifth Schedule para 3: permission is needed for, among others, (e) refusing to strike out, (f) appeals only about costs,
-- (h) REFUSING permission to amend, (i) GIVING security for costs, (j) discovery, (k) REFUSING a stay, (l) most other
-- interlocutory orders.
--
-- Two mirrored pairs are treated differently. Giving permission to amend: no appeal. Refusing it: appeal with permission.
-- Refusing security for costs: no appeal. Giving it: appeal with permission. In each pair, the order that lets the case
-- go on unencumbered is final; the one that obstructs it can be appealed.
DECLARE GDOrder IS ONE OF
    `a judgment after trial`
    `a consent judgment`
    `an order giving permission to amend`
    `an order refusing permission to amend`
    `an order refusing security for costs`
    `an order giving security for costs`
    `an order on further and better particulars`
    `an order on interrogatories`
    `an order refusing to strike out`
    `an order striking out the action`
    `an order on discovery`
    `an order refusing a stay`
    `an order for summary judgment`
    `a decision on costs only`

GIVEN `the order` IS A GDOrder
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the order is never appealable` IF
    CONSIDER `the order`
    WHEN `a consent judgment` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an order giving permission to amend` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an order refusing security for costs` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an order on further and better particulars` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an order on interrogatories` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the order` IS A GDOrder
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the order needs permission by its kind` IF
    CONSIDER `the order`
    WHEN `an order refusing permission to amend` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an order giving security for costs` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an order refusing to strike out` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an order on discovery` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an order refusing a stay` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a decision on costs only` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE GDAppeal HAS
    `the order` IS A GDOrder
    `amount in dispute` IS A NUMBER
    `exclusive original jurisdiction under a written law, or Family Division at first instance` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the parties agreed in writing the decision is final` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the appellant alleges fraud, illegality or a fundamental breach of natural justice` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the appeal` IS A GDAppeal
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `an appeal can be brought at all` IF
        NOT `the order is never appealable` (`the appeal`'s `the order`)
    AND (    NOT `the appeal`'s `the parties agreed in writing the decision is final`
         OR  `the appeal`'s `the appellant alleges fraud, illegality or a fundamental breach of natural justice`)

GIVEN `the appeal` IS A GDAppeal
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `permission is needed to appeal from the General Division` IF
        `an appeal can be brought at all` `the appeal`
    AND (    `the order needs permission by its kind` (`the appeal`'s `the order`)
         OR  `the appeal`'s `the parties agreed in writing the decision is final`
         OR  (    `the appeal`'s `amount in dispute` AT MOST 250000
              AND NOT `the appeal`'s `exclusive original jurisdiction under a written law, or Family Division at first instance`))

§§ `29C and the Sixth Schedule -- which appellate court`

-- s 29C(1): an appeal from the General Division goes to the APPELLATE DIVISION, (2) unless the Sixth Schedule or another
-- written law sends it to the COURT OF APPEAL. (3): s 29C "does not create any right of appeal".
-- Sixth Schedule para 1: to the Court of Appeal where "(a) the appeal arises from a case relating to constitutional or
-- administrative law (EVEN IF THE APPEAL DOES NOT RAISE ANY ISSUE relating to" it); likewise (b) contempt, (c)
-- arbitration, (d) corporate insolvency, (e) patents, (ea) admiralty and shipping; (f) appeals from the SICC; (g), (h)
-- election cases; (i) appeals under listed sections, among them "section 18(5) of the Maintenance of Parents Act 1995";
-- (k), (l) mediation.
--
-- The route follows the subject of the CASE, not the issue on appeal. A costs appeal in an arbitration-related case goes to
-- the Court of Appeal although it raises no point of arbitration law. And an elderly parent's maintenance appeal under the
-- Maintenance of Parents Act goes to the apex court, where a $10 million contract appeal goes to the Appellate Division.
DECLARE CaseSubject IS ONE OF
    `constitutional or administrative law`
    `contempt of court`
    `arbitration`
    `corporate insolvency`
    `patents`
    `admiralty or shipping`
    `an SICC decision`
    `a parliamentary or presidential election`
    `an appeal under section 18(5) of the Maintenance of Parents Act`
    `mediation`
    `contract`
    `tort`
    `land`
    `family`

GIVEN `the subject` IS A CaseSubject
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the appeal goes to the Court of Appeal` IF
    CONSIDER `the subject`
    WHEN `contract` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `tort` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `land` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `family` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `the subject` IS A CaseSubject, `the issue on appeal concerns that subject` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the routing depends on the issue on appeal` IF
    FALSE

GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 29C creates a right of appeal` IF
    FALSE

§§ `22(7) -- relief for a party who did not appeal`

-- s 22(7): on an appeal from a lower court, the General Division's powers "(a) may be exercised in relation to any part of
-- the decision ..., including any part ... to which the appeal does not relate; and (b) may be exercised in favour of any
-- party ..., including any party ... who has not appealed".
GIVEN `the party appealed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the General Division may vary the decision in the party's favour` IF
    TRUE

§§ `45, 60C -- no automatic stay`

-- s 60C(1) (and s 45 for the Appellate Division): an appeal "does not operate as a stay of execution or enforcement ...
-- unless the court whose decision is appealed against or the Court of Appeal so orders".
GIVEN `an appeal is pending` IS A BOOLEAN, `a court ordered a stay` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the judgment may be enforced` IF
    NOT `a court ordered a stay`
